Final Reflection
Did your prompt improve from Version 1 to Version 4? What changes made the biggest difference?
Yes, the output quality improved dramatically. The most impactful changes were introducing explicit structural constraints (requiring a tabular breakdown, specific deliverables, and budget numbers) and providing clear operational parameters (20,000 students, SSO integration, $50k budget).

Overall Reflection
What makes a prompt effective?
An effective prompt provides clear context, defines a precise role or persona, specifies constraints, and explicitly lists required output formats or structural components.

Which prompting technique did you find most useful?
Role Prompting combined with Constraint Specification. Giving the AI a specific persona (e.g., Business Analyst, Project Manager) shifts its context window to adopt industry-standard terms and analytical frameworks.

Why is context important when working with AI?
AI models generate text based on statistical probability. Supplying rich context (e.g., team size, budget limits, user base) narrows down vague possibilities, ensuring the AI produces customized, actionable solutions rather than generic advice.

Why should we review AI-generated responses instead of accepting them automatically?
AI models can introduce hallucinations, miscategorize logic, or include assumptions not grounded in project reality (as seen in Exercise C). Human oversight guarantees factual correctness, domain alignment, and safety.

How can AI support software engineers across the SDLC?
AI serves as an assistant in every SDLC phase: parsing raw customer briefs into requirements (Planning), drafting architecture diagrams and code boilerplate (Design & Coding), writing test cases (QA), generating infrastructure scripts (Deployment), and analyzing error logs (Maintenance).
